##Introduction##

This directory contains all the out of tree device tree structures, for external
(also called out of tree) modules, along with Makefiles to support the build.
Objective is to build the external device trees, with the same build tools being
used by mainline kernel. All the modules specific device tree source files, are
to be added under this directory. Each such child directory contains module
specific device tree files, typically one dts file, many dtsi files, and
optionally, module specific DT binding files. Each module specific directory also
contains its own Makefile, needed to build it.

The top 'devicetree' directory contains Makefile, containing required rules to build
all the device tree source files(*.dts and *.dtsi files).

devicetree
|
|---> Makefile, Makefile.common. These files add core build framework.
|--->Board/Soc specific files: *.dts(per board/soc), one or more *.dtsi,
and *.h header files for deviectree bindings).

###Adding a new module#####
The main concept behind adding new module is almost same as in the standard
Linux kernel device tree. For instance, nearly same procedure is to be
followed, as one would following while adding new vendor specific directory,
under following mainline kernel directory:
<kernel_dir>/arch/<ARCH_NAME>/boot/dts.

Steps to be followed:

1. Add the new *.dts file, if we are adding the support of overlay for new board/soc,
and add the DTS file related task into the Makefile as following:
	dtb-y += <new_DTS_file_name>.dtbo

Else, if the DTS file is already present, add the due modification in the same for overlay.

Optionally, add dtsi files as appropriate. If the module used its own DT binding header files,
create binding-directory and copy the header files under the same.

2. If the module needs its own DT binding, add the patch of the DT bindings in
file Devicetree/Makefile.common, by adding following line:
TECHPACK_INCLUDE += <path of module specific DT binding>

###Building the device tree bins for out of tree/external modules###

make KDIR=<main_kernel_directory> ARCH=<arch_name(arm64 by default)> dtbs

Prerequisite: The kernel directory pointed by KDIR, should be built to the
extent of generating .config file.
